Day 10 of my DevOps Troubleshooting Journey

🔥 Real-Time Scenario: Kubernetes Pods Are Running, But Users Are Getting 503 Errors

Imagine this situation:

The application suddenly becomes unavailable and users start reporting:

❌ 503 Service Unavailable

You check Kubernetes:

`kubectl get pods`

And surprisingly, all pods are:

✅ Running

So, what could be wrong?

Here’s how I would troubleshoot it as a DevOps Engineer 👇

🔍 Step 1: Check the Service

`kubectl get svc`

`kubectl describe svc <service-name>`

Verify:
• Service exists
• Correct targetPort
• Correct selector
• Endpoints are available

🔍 Step 2: Check Service Endpoints

`kubectl get endpoints <service-name>`

If you see:

`ENDPOINTS: <none>`

The Service is not finding the required pods.

I would then compare the Service selector with the pod labels:

`kubectl get pods --show-labels`

🔍 Step 3: Test the Application Inside the Pod

`kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- curl localhost:<port>`

If the application responds successfully, the issue may be with the Service or networking layer.

🔍 Step 4: Check Readiness Probes

`kubectl describe pod <pod-name>`

I would look for:

`Readiness probe failed`

A pod can be in:

🟢 Running state

but still be:

🔴 Not Ready

When a readiness probe fails, Kubernetes can remove that pod from the Service endpoints.

🔍 Step 5: Check Ingress

If the application is exposed through an Ingress:

`kubectl get ingress`

`kubectl describe ingress <ingress-name>`

I would verify:
• Correct backend Service
• Correct Service port
• Hostname configuration
• Ingress Controller health

🧠 Key Learning:

A Kubernetes pod being "Running" does NOT always mean the application is available to users.

For a 503 issue, I follow the request flow:

👤 User
⬇️
🌐 Load Balancer / Ingress
⬇️
⚙️ Service
⬇️
🔗 Endpoints
⬇️
📦 Pod
⬇️
🖥️ Application

The important thing is to troubleshoot each layer systematically rather than immediately restarting pods.

💡 Interview Tip:

If asked:

"Pods are Running, but users are getting 503. What will you check?"

My answer:

"I would first check the Service and its endpoints, verify that the Service selector matches the pod labels, check readiness probes, and then verify the Ingress or Load Balancer configuration. Finally, I would test connectivity directly to the pod."

Every production issue is an opportunity to learn something new. 🚀
